From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Moraza Sanchez <dmorazasanchezmendo@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 25 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] radv,nir,aco: BC-250 FSR4 deferred SDot lowering
 (EXP-042B / V3)

Speed up FSR4's INT8 path on GFX1013. The hardware has no working packed
signed dot product, so sdot_4x8_iadd falls back to software. Three things
make that fallback much cheaper:

1. A dense-reduction prepass expanding the largest FSR4 reduction kernels
   into signed 24-bit multiply-accumulate chains.
2. The same MAD-chain form in the NIR algebraic fallback, via
   sdot_4x8_a_b_madacc and sdot_4x8_a_b_madconst, with matching
   nir_op_imad24_ir3 and nir_op_imul24 support in ACO instruction
   selection.
3. A deferred lowering round: remaining packed SDots are held through one
   full NIR optimization pass before the real capability set is restored
   and they are lowered. Keeping the SDot intact lets upstream's own
   iadd(sdot(a, b, 0), c) -> sdot(a, b, c) fold absorb FSR4's accumulator
   wrapper, which shortens the live ranges that were spilling.

The MAD chain costs *more* instructions than the balanced/add3 form,
because v_mad_i32_i24 is VOP3 and cannot take SDWA byte selects, so each
byte needs its own extract. It is faster anyway: the chain keeps one
accumulator live where the add3 form needs four products live at once, and
on these shaders register pressure dominates instruction count. Do not
"simplify" this back to add3 on instruction-count grounds.

Tested with FSR 4.1.1 in Cyberpunk 2077: ~4 ms OptiScaler latency and
~89 fps, against ~6 ms and roughly native framerate without it.

Based on EXP-042B from BC-250 FSR4 testing (David Moraza Sanchez,
dmorazasanchez/bc250-fsr4, `v3` branch,
https://github.com/dmorazasanchez/bc250-fsr4/tree/v3), reduced to the
pieces that measurably matter by bisecting on real hardware:

 - The GFX1013 compute-queue/identity changes and the RADV_GFX103 override
   are dropped: they duplicate 0001 and 0004 byte-for-byte.
 - The LDS spill-slot override in aco_spill.cpp is dropped. Restoring it
   alone to an otherwise-trimmed build reproduced the full regression, so
   it earns nothing, and it deliberately traded occupancy away.
 - The explicit iadd(0, OpSDot) wrapper rules are dropped. Upstream
   already folds iadd(a, 0) -> a and iadd(sdot(a, b, 0), c), both ungated,
   so they still run during the deferred round.

Verified against a positive control: a testing driver built from a
byte-identical copy of the full patch measured ~4.2 ms and ~89 fps,
confirming the comparison harness reproduced the known-good result.
---
